  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n\nThis problem is a nonconvex optimization task on a finite-dimensional space of step function representations. The goal is to minimize the maximum overlap C_5, which computes the worst-case overlap between two shifted step functions. The challenge lies in balancing the integral constraint (sum(h) * dx = 1) and avoiding regions of high overlap at any shift.\n\nThe centralized approach involves:\n- Multi-resolution sequential optimization across 45 and 90-point grids\n- Leveraging the provided initial parent solution for both resolutions\n- Using a combination of derivative-based and derivative-free optimization\n- Incorporating a local search strategy to escape shallow optima\n\nKey rationale draws from harmonic analysis:\n- The problem resembles finding a function with reduced correlation across shifts\n- The maximum overlap constraint resembles a uniformity requirement across scales\n- Breaking the function into smaller-resolution components may expose better representations\n\nImportant limitations:\n- Coefficient-based solvers may struggle with non-convex objective\n- Ad-hoc repair mechanisms are needed for constraint maintenance\n- Gradient computation requires careful handling of the max individual objective\n- Numerical instability may occur at extreme resolutions\n\nThis approach aims to build upon the initial parent while proposing a theoretically sound local improvement strategy derived from integral constraints.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n\nRepresentation:\n- h \u2208 [0,1]^n_points\n- dx = 2.0/n_points\n- Integral constraint: sum(h) * dx = 1\n\nObjective:\n- C_5(h) = max_{0\u2264k<n_points} (np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\")[k] * dx)\n- The correlation array has length 2*n_points-1\n\nDual formulation:\n- We minimize the worst-case asymptotic autocorrelation at any shift\n- Creates a more uniform distribution under shift operations\n\nMathematical creation:\n- For any candidate h, the autocorrelation array A(k) = \u222bh(x)h(x+k)dx\n- We seek to minimize the maximum of (A(k) * dx) for k from 0 to n_points-1\n- The integral constraint doubles as a form of normalization\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n\nStep 1: Pre-initialization\n- n_point_resolutions = [45, 90] (optional: can add more grid sizes)\n- Validate existing parent if available\n- For current run, determine initial points for each resolution:\n  * For 45-point: use initial_h_values if available, otherwise uniform initializing\n  * For 90-point: use cubic interpolation of 45-point to 90-point grid\n\nStep 2: Core optimization with validation\nFor each resolution (from coarse to fine):\n  A. Construct optimized object incorporating both grid and coefficients\n  B. Apply dimension-moderating technique (here: summed squared gradient descent at resolution 45 and hypersphere projection at 90) to maintain boundaries\n  C. Implement custom repair constraint handler that redistributes integral mass\n  D. Local search mechanism: attack constraint boundaries by exploring gradient-descent steps around corners\n  E. Maintain archive of improving solutions regardless of resolution\n\nStep 3: Detail-oriented execution\n- Score(h) = C_5(h) computed via discrete correlation\n- Repair function:\n    def repair(h):\n        s = sum(h)\n        if abs(s - n_points/2) > 1.0:\n            over = min(1, s - n_points/2/2)\n            under = min(1, n_points/2/2 - s)\n            h = h / s * (n_points/2) if s != 0 else 0.5 * np.ones(len(h))\n            h = h * 0.99**len(h)  # exponential backoff\n            return h + (1 - np.clip(s + over - under, 0, 1)) * np.clip(uhrum, 0, 1)\n- This repair ensures bounding box and integrates to 1 within tolerance\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n\nDetermination of grid sizes:\n- Primary: 45-point (given parent)\n- Secondary: 90-point (for resolution increase)\n\nOptimization parameters:\n- Learning rates (gradient) between 0.1 to 0.5\n- Minimal penetration depth: 1/ (n_points * n_points)\n- Transaction boundaries (gradient-associated attack): |\u2207C_5 * step| < tolerance\n- Entropy minimization/maximization as secondary scoring should be excluded as per task constraints\n\nProgression to neighbors:\n- Step function mutation at 45-point grid: swap coefficients but maintain \u222b=1\n- At 90-point grid: introduce Wilcoxon-distance-evaluated neighborhood coefficients\n- Substitute random uniform exploration with quadratic interpolation between archived points\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n\nDominant costs:\n- 90-point grid requires 90 * 90 * 24 = 194400 floating point operations worst-case\n- COBYLA calls: 100 iterations maximum with 20 function evaluations per call per instance\n- Repair function: runs at worst 10 times per function evaluation (2 during repair)\n\nExecution strategy:\n- Iterate between coarse and fine resolution optimization\n- Terminate early if random walk improves objective below threshold\n- COBYLA with maximum iterations=500 allows termination after 30 seconds\n- If 45-point unresolved, promote to 90-point and vice versa\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n\nEach candidate h must satisfy:\n1. Score(h) = np correlative maxima\n2. \u222bh = 1 (dx scaled sum)\n3. h values \u2208 [0,1]\n4. np.isfinite(Score(h))\n\nFinal ejection checks:\n- For final_candidate at resolution 90-point: recompute correlation at multiple grid points to mitigate steepest-descent artifacts\n- Use guard band of tolerance=1e-9\n- Resume optimization if gradient descent fails to validate repair\n\n Return format:\n- (h_values, final_c5, n_points) with final_c5 = Score(final_candidate)\n- If constrained to 45-n_points, only use 45-point path\n\nThis plan ensures systematic progression from given parent to improved states while carefully incorporating mathematical structure inherent in the optimization problem.",
